l of Science and Engineering, Saitama University, Saitama, Japan\n\nMasatsugu Toyota\n\n  5. Department of Botany, University of Wisconsin, Madison, WI, USA\n\nMasatsugu Toyota\n\n## Supplementary information\n\n### Supplementary Information (download PDF )\n\nSupplementary Figs. 1–8, Table 1, Videos 1–9 and references.\n\n### Reporting Summary (download PDF )\n\n### Supplementary Video 1 (download MOV )\n\nThe [Ca^{2+}]_{cyt} increases following mechanical stimulation of a sensory hair of the GCaMP6f-transgenic Dionaea. The video is played at real time speed. Time (seconds, s) after the first mechanical stimulus is indicated. Scale bar, 2 mm.\n\n## Source data\n\n### Source Data Fig. 3 (download XLSX )\n\nStatistical source data.\n\n### Source Data Extended Data Fig. 3 (download XLSX )\n\nStatistical source data.\n\n### Source Data Table 1 (download XLSX )\n\nStatistical source data.\n\n## About this article\n\n### Cite this article\n\nSuda, H., Mano, H., Toyota, M. et al. Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap. Nat. Plants 6, 1219–1224 (2020). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41477-020-00773-1\n\n  * Received: 16 February 2020\n\n  * Accepted: 24 August 2020\n\n  * Published: 05 October 2020\n\n  * Version of record: 05 October 2020\n\n  * Issue date: October 2020\n\n  * DOI (Digital Object Identifier): https://doi.org/10.1038/s41477-020-00773-1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDYSCALCULIA, a Venus flytrap mutant without the ability to count action potentials - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0960982222019959)\nciteturn14search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; DYSCALCULIA, a Venus flytrap mutant without the ability to count action potentials ... DYSC exhibits defects in trap-restricted calcium- and JA-associated processes ... ^{1}^{,}^{2}^{,}^{3} A first AP is associated with a subcritical rise in cytosolic calcium concentration, but when the second AP arrives in time, calcium levels pass the threshold required for fast trap closure.\n\n## Current Biology\n\nDate: 6 February 2023\n\nPages: 589-596.e5\n\nVolume: Volume 33, Issue 3\n\nView accessibility information\n\n### Published by\n\nShow more\n\nShort communication\n\nOpen archive\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\n# \n\nReport\n\nDYSCALCULIA, a Venus flytrap mutant without the ability to count action potentials\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Anda-Larisa Iosip ^{1} ^{4}, Sönke Scherzer ^{1}, Sonja Bauer ^{1}, Dirk Becker ^{1}, Markus Krischke ^{2}, Khaled A.S. Al-Rasheid ^{3}, Jörg Schultz ^{4}, Ines Kreuzer ^{1} ^{5}, Rainer Hedrich ^{1}\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.cub.2022.12.058\n\n  * Article\n\n## Highlights\n\n  * •\n\nThe Dionaea mutant DYSC fires action potentials but does not snap close its traps\n\n  * •\n\nDYSC exhibits defects in trap-restricted calcium- and JA-associated processes\n\n  * •\n\nTouch-activation of calcium signaling is largely suppressed in DYSC traps\n\n  * •\n\nDYSC is impaired in decoding touch-/action potential-induced calcium signals\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Venus flytrap Dionaea muscipula estimates prey nutrient content by counting trigger hair contacts initiating action potentials (APs) and calcium waves traveling all over the trap.^{1}^{,}^{2}^{,}^{3} A first AP is associated with a subcritical rise in cytosolic calcium concentration, but when the second AP arrives in time, calcium levels pass the threshold required for fast trap closure. Consequently, memory function and decision-making are timed via a calcium clock.^{3}^{,}^{4} For higher numbers of APs elicited by the struggling prey, the Ca^{2+} clock connects to the networks governed by the touch hormone jasmonic acid (JA), which initiates slow, hermetic trap sealing and mining of the animal food stock.^{5} Two distinct phases of trap closure can be distinguished within Dionaea’s hunting cycle: (1) very fast trap snapping requiring two APs and crossing of a critical cytosolic Ca^{2+} level and (2) JA-dependent slow trap sealing and prey processing induced by more than five APs. The Dionaea mutant DYSC is still able to fire touch-induced APs but does not snap close its traps and fails to enter the hunting cycle after prolonged mechanostimulation. Transcriptomic analyses revealed that upon trigger hair touch/AP stimulation, activation of calcium signaling is largely suppressed in DYSC traps. The observation that external JA application restored hunting cycle progression together with the DYSC phenotype and its transcriptional landscape indicates that DYSC cannot properly read, count, and decode touch/AP-induced calcium signals that are key in prey capture and processing.\n\n## Keywords\n\naction potential\n\n;\n\nVenus flytrap\n\n;\n\nDionaea muscipula\n\n;\n\ncounting in plants\n\n;\n\ncalcium signaling\n\n;\n\ntrap closure\n\n;\n\ncarnivorous plants\n\n;\n\nDionaea cultivars\n\n;\n\nplant mechanosensation\n\n## Data and code availability\n\n  * •\n\nRNA-seq data have been deposited at SRA and are publicly available as of the date of publication. Accession numbers are listed in the key resources table.\n\n  * •\n\nOther data and code have been deposited at GitHub under the following link and is publicly available as of the date of publication: https://github.com/iannda/DIANE_Diomu_DYSC.\n\n  * •\n\nAny additional information required to reanalyze the data reported in this paper is available from the lead contact upon request.\n\n^{5}\n    \n\nLead contact--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCalcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33020606/)\nciteturn14search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrapHiraku Suda^{  1   2 }, Hiroaki Mano^{  1   2   3 }, Masatsugu Toyota^{  4   5 }, Kenji Fukushima^{  1   2   6 }, Tetsuro Mimura^{  7 }, Izuo Tsutsui^{  8 }, Rainer Hedrich^{  6   9 }, Yosuke Tamada^{  1   2   10 }, Mitsuyasu Hasebe^{  11   12 }\n\n. 2020 Oct;6(10):1219-1224.\n\ndoi: 10.1038/s41477-020-00773-1. Epub 2020 Oct 5.\n\n# Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap\n\nHiraku Suda^{  1   2 }, Hiroaki Mano^{  1   2   3 }, Masatsugu Toyota^{  4   5 }, Kenji Fukushima^{  1   2   6 }, Tetsuro Mimura^{  7 }, Izuo Tsutsui^{  8 }, Rainer Hedrich^{  6   9 }, Yosuke Tamada^{  1   2   10 }, Mitsuyasu Hasebe^{  11   12 }\n\n### Affiliations\n\n  * ^{1} National Institute for Basic Biology, Okazaki, Japan.\n  * ^{2} Department of Basic Biology, The Graduate University for Advanced Studies (SOKENDAI), Okazaki, Japan.\n  * ^{3} JST, PRESTO, Saitama, Japan.\n  * ^{4} Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Graduate School of Science and Engineering, Saitama University, Saitama, Japan.\n  * ^{5} Department of Botany, University of Wisconsin, Madison, WI, USA.\n  * ^{6} Institute for Molecular Plant Physiology and Biophysics, University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany.\n  * ^{7} Department of Biology, Graduate School of Science, Kobe University, Kobe, Japan.\n  * ^{8} Graduate School of Business Administration, Hitotsubashi University, Kunitachi, Japan.\n  * ^{9} Zoology Department, College of Science, King Saud University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia.\n  * ^{10} School of Engineering, Utsunomiya University, Utsunomiya, Japan.\n  * ^{11} National Institute for Basic Biology, Okazaki, Japan. mhasebe@nibb.ac.jp.\n  * ^{12} Department of Basic Biology, The Graduate University for Advanced Studies (SOKENDAI), Okazaki, Japan. mhasebe@nibb.ac.jp.\n\n  * PMID: 33020606\n\n# Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap\n\nHiraku Suda et al. Nat Plants. 2020 Oct.\n\n\n. 2020 Oct;6(10):1219-1224.\n\ndoi: 10.1038/s41477-020-00773-1. Epub 2020 Oct 5.\n\n### Authors\n\nHiraku Suda^{  1   2 }, Hiroaki Mano^{  1   2   3 }, Masatsugu Toyota^{  4   5 }, Kenji Fukushima^{  1   2   6 }, Tetsuro Mimura^{  7 }, Izuo Tsutsui^{  8 }, Rainer Hedrich^{  6   9 }, Yosuke Tamada^{  1   2   10 }, Mitsuyasu Hasebe^{  11   12 }\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe leaves of the carnivorous plant Venus flytrap, Dionaea muscipula (Dionaea) close rapidly to capture insect prey. The closure response usually requires two successive mechanical stimuli to sensory hairs on the leaf blade within approximately 30 s (refs. ^{1-4}). An unknown biological system in Dionaea is thought to memorize the first stimulus and transduce the signal from the sensory hair to the leaf blade^{2}. Here, we link signal memory to calcium dynamics using transgenic Dionaea expressing a Ca^{2+} sensor. Stimulation of a sensory hair caused an increase in cytosolic Ca^{2+} concentration ([Ca^{2+}]_{cyt}) starting in the sensory hair and spreading to the leaf blade. A second stimulus increased [Ca^{2+}]_{cyt} to an even higher level, meeting a threshold that is correlated to the leaf blade closure. Because [Ca^{2+}]_{cyt} gradually decreased after the first stimulus, the [Ca^{2+}]_{cyt} increase induced by the second stimulus was insufficient to meet the putative threshold for movement after about 30 s. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVolume 6 Issue 10, October 2020 (https://www.nature.com/nplants/volumes/6/issues/10)\nciteturn14search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; See Suda, H. et al. ...   * ### Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrapA transgenic Venus flytrap expressing a fluorescent calcium sensor allows real-time live quantification of calcium waves triggered by sensory hair movement.\nThis epigenetic cold memory is maternally inherited to the zytote and early embryos.\n\n    * Xiao Luo\n    * Yang Ou\n    * Yuehui He\n\nLetter 21 Sept 2020\n\n  * ### Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap\n\nA transgenic Venus flytrap expressing a fluorescent calcium sensor allows real-time live quantification of calcium waves triggered by sensory hair movement. The study suggests that calcium levels represent the molecular basis for the memory effect that requires two stimulations within 30 s.\n\n    * Hiraku Suda\n    * Hiroaki Mano\n    * Mitsuyasu Hasebe\n\nLetter 05 Oct 2020\n\n  * ### Empirical evidence for resilience of tropical forest photosynthesis in a warmer world\n\nPhotosynthesis in tropical forests shows an apparent sensitivity to temperature. This Letter teases apart the effects of temperature and correlated atmospheric water demand on ecosystem productivity.\n\n    * Marielle N. Smith\n    * Tyeen C. Taylor\n    * Scott R. Saleska\n\nLetter 12 Oct 2020\n\n  * ### A scoping review of adoption of climate-resilient crops by small-scale producers in low- and middle-income countries\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRepelling insects with calcium | Nature Plants (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41477-022-01318-4)\nciteturn14search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; Plants 6, 1219–1224; 2020), a transgenic Venus flytrap expressing a fluorescent calcium biosensor revealed why two successive stimuli during a 30-s memory period are necessary to trap juicy wandering insects and not inert debris. ... Published in Nature Communications, Hagihara and colleagues measured cytosolic calcium levels simultaneously with long-distance electrical signals, using electrodes placed along the leaf axis.\n\n  * Research Highlight\n  * Published: 05 December 2022\n\nPlant movements\n\n# Repelling insects with calcium\n\n  * Guillaume Tena^{1} \n\nNature Plants volume 8, page 1331 (2022)\n\n\nRecently in our pages (Nat. Plants 6, 1219–1224; 2020), a transgenic Venus flytrap expressing a fluorescent calcium biosensor revealed why two successive stimuli during a 30-s memory period are necessary to trap juicy wandering insects and not inert debris. Now, from the same teams in Japan, comes a transgenic Mimosa pudica that uses the same biosensor to once again monitor calcium dynamics in real time. Published in Nature Communications, Hagihara and colleagues measured cytosolic calcium levels simultaneously with long-distance electrical signals, using electrodes placed along the leaf axis. Characterization of calcium and electrical signal propagation demonstrate that they are spatiotemporally coupled and trigger synchronized leaflet closure in seconds. The shape of the response is also specific to the stimulus (touch or wounding).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCalcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap | Article Information | J-GLOBAL (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/en/detail?JGLOBAL_ID=202002229289817297)\nciteturn14search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.8 years ago; Crawled: today; # Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap ... Suda Hiraku\n\nArt\n\nJ-GLOBAL ID：202002229289817297   Reference number：20A2345418\n\n# Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap\n\nトランスジェニックVenusハエトラップで可視化したトラップ閉鎖中のカルシウム動態〖JST・京大機械翻訳〗\n\nAuthor (19)：\n\nSuda Hiraku\n\nAbout Suda Hiraku\n\n  * Search \"202250000843713944\"\n  * Search \"Suda Hiraku\"\n\n(National Institute for Basic Biology, Okazaki, Japan)\n\nAbout National Institute for Basic Biology, Okazaki, Japan\n\n  * Search \"201951000140879733\"\n  * Search \"National Institute for Basic Biology, Okazaki, Japan\"\n  * Detailed information\n\n, \n\nSuda Hiraku\n\nAbout Suda Hiraku\n\n  * Search \"202250000843713944\"\n  * Search \"Suda Hiraku\"\n\n(Department of Basic Biology, The Graduate University for Advanced Studies (SOKENDAI), Okazaki, Japan)\n\nAbout Department of Basic Biology, The Graduate University for