resholded excitability with short-term physiological memory: two temporally coincident mechanical stimuli produce calcium-mediated depolarizations that summate above a threshold for hydraulic actuation, whereas isolated stimuli decay below threshold within ∼20 s. This pattern reflects electrophysiological integration rather than cognitive processing or decision-making in the psychological sense. A quantitative reinterpretation of published data shows that Dionaea\n\n## Ethical Compliance\n\nNo experiments involving humans or animals were performed.\n\n## Funding\n\nNo external funding was received.\n\n## CRediT authorship contribution statement\n\nTahir Rahman: Writing – original draft, Conceptualization.\n\n## Declaration of Generative AI and AI-assisted technologies in the writing process\n\nLanguage and formatting were refined using OpenAI’s ChatGPT under author supervision. All scientific interpretation and conclusions are original and verified by the author.\n\n## Declaration of Competing Interest\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDemystifying the Venus flytrap action potential - Hedrich - 2023 - New Phytologist - Wiley Online Library (https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.19113)\nciteturn14search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; Crawled: last week; The cytosolic Ca^{2+} transients of the first and second wave sum up, and upon reaching a critical threshold, they induce the release of viscoelastic energy stored in the open trap, making the trap snap shut (Suda et al., 2020). ... Trigger hairs located at the inner trap surface serve as mechanosensors: their displacement provokes the action potential. (b) Upon touch perception, calcium waves and action potentials (APs) are released and together propagate along both trap lobes. (c) Dionaea's AP consists of five distinct phases: after an initial Ca^{2+} influx via DmGLR3.6, the Ca^{2+} signal is amplified by a Ca^{2+}-induced Ca^{2+} release (CICR) from the endoplasmic reticulum to an all-or-nothing cytosolic Ca^{2+} transient (phase 1). ... During membrane hyperpolarization, DmAHA4 voltage inactivates and H^{+}-driven DmHAK5 and NRT-type anion transporters reset the pre-AP resting potential by recharging the K^{+} and anion battery (phase 5). (d) Two APs drive the cytosolic Ca^{2+} level above the threshold upon which rapid trap closure is unavoidable.\nNegative of −60 mV DmSKOR inactivates, slowing the fast, initial repolarization (2^{nd} shoulder). The delayed deactivation of the depolarization-activated H^{+} pump results in sustained H^{+} export, thus provoking a transient overshoot hyperpolarization (phase 4). During membrane hyperpolarization, DmAHA4 voltage inactivates and H^{+}-driven DmHAK5 and NRT-type anion transporters reset the pre-AP resting potential by recharging the K^{+} and anion battery (phase 5). (d) Two APs drive the cytosolic Ca^{2+} level above the threshold upon which rapid trap closure is unavoidable. Snap buckling is achieved by the release of K^{+} and anions and, in turn, turgor loss in the motor cells of the inner leaflet. As a result, the metastable hydrostatic pressure balance between inner and outer leaflet collapses and the open concave trap geometry flips over to the convex closed one.\n\nThe Venus flytrap Dyscalculia (DYSC) mutant cannot properly count and decode the Ca^{2+}-APs and is thus unable to snap close its trap and to produce sufficient JA in response to prolonged mechanostimulation. The JA-dependent steps of the hunting cycle can, however, be rescued by external JA application in DYSC (Iosip et al., 2023). This shows that prey capture, recognition and processing are under the control of the Ca^{2+} and the JA clock.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - CDENewsVol4NoS5_Text1.docx (https://www.constrained-evo.shigenobulab.org/docs/CDENewsVol4NoS5s.pdf)\nciteturn14search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.9 years ago; Calcium dynamics during trap closure visualized ... Hiraku Suda, Hiroaki Mano, Masatsugu Toyota, Kenji Fukushima,\nCalcium dynamics during trap closure visualized \nin transgenic Venus flytrap  \n \nHiraku Suda, Hiroaki Mano, Masatsugu Toyota, Kenji Fukushima, \nTetsuro Mimura, Izuo Tsutsui, Rainer Hedrich, Yosuke Tamada, \nMitsuyasu Hasebe \n \nNature Plants (2020) 6, 1219-1224 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1038/s41477-020-00773-1  \n \nThe leaves of the carnivorous plant Venus flytrap, Dionaea muscipula (Dionaea) close \nrapidly to capture insect prey. The closure response requires two successive mechanical \nstimuli to sensory hairs on the leaf blade within approximately 30 seconds. An unknown \nbiological system in Dionaea is thought to memorize the first stimulus and transduce the \nsignal from the sensory hair to the leaf blade. Here, we link signal memory to calcium \ndynamics using transgenic Dionaea expressing a Ca2+ sensor. Stimulation of a sensory hair \ncaused an increase in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration ([Ca2+]cyt) starting in the sensory hair \nand spreading to the leaf blade. A second stimulus increased [Ca2+]cyt to an even higher \nlevel, meeting a threshold that is correlated to the leaf blade closure. Because [Ca2+]cyt \ngradually decreased after the first stimulus, the [Ca2+]cyt increase induced by the second \nstimulus was insufficient to meet the putative threshold for movement after about 30 \nseconds. The Ca2+ wave triggered by mechanical stimulation moved an order of magnitude \nfaster than that induced by wounding in petioles of Arabidopsis thaliana4 and Dionaea. \nThe capacity for rapid movement has evolved repeatedly in flowering plants. This study \nopens a path to investigate the role of Ca2+ in plant movement mechanisms and their \nevolution. \n \n \n \n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCalcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap - 科研通 (https://www.ablesci.com/assist/d